<header class="main-header">
  <div class="series-title">Likutay Halachos · Choshen Mishpat</div>
  <h1>Hilchos Uraiv 5 — Part 2</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">Laws of a Guarantor — 5 (§§30–36) · Based on "Teekoo" — Likutay Moharan II:1 · The journeys, the Mishkan, elevating the Makom, and Rosh HaShanah</div>
</header>

<p class="source" style="text-align:center; margin-bottom: 30px;">Based on the Torah "Teekoo" 1 in Likutay Tinyana, Siman 1. Continuing from Part 1 (§§23–29).</p>

<div class="section-header">§30</div>
<p>And therefore all <span class="key-term">kinyanim</span> are derived from the verse: <span class="scripture">"And this was formerly done in Israel… a man would remove his shoe"</span> <span class="source">(Ruth 4:7)</span> etc. — which was said at the time when Boaz married Rus, from whom was born David, from whom <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span> will come forth. For he will rectify everything and elevate everything to its root, where everything is truth and there is no change — from which all <span class="key-term">kinyanim</span> are drawn, whereby one keeps his word and no longer changes, as above.</p>

<div class="section-header">§31</div>
<p>And therefore before <span class="key-term">Shavuos</span>, which is the receiving of the Torah, we read Parashas B'midbar, in which they were commanded to count the Children of Israel when the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> was erected — as our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, gave as a mnemonic: "Count and conclude" [<span class="aramaic">m'nu v'itzru</span>]. For each year every one of Israel must draw upon himself the holiness of the receiving of the Torah on <span class="key-term">Shavuos</span> — through which he will come to the ultimate purpose for which the world was created, which is that he should have dominion over the angels. But one must see to it that this dominion should endure — that the angels who are jealous of this should not cast him down, <span class="key-term">chas v'shalom</span>. And for this one must grasp himself in the <span class="key-term">Keesay HaKavod</span>, which is the aspect of the roots of the souls of Israel, the aspect of <span class="scripture">"the mother of all life"</span> etc.</p>
<p>And therefore we read before this Parashas B'midbar, which is the counting of the Children of Israel — for they were commanded to count the heads of the Children of Israel, to gather and assemble them all, to bring them in number and count — in order to arouse all the roots of their souls, which are hewn from the <span class="key-term">Keesay HaKavod</span>, in order to grasp oneself in them, in order to be saved from the jealousy of the angels. And therefore they were commanded to count them by the number of their names — for the name is the soul, as it is written: <span class="scripture">"A living soul — that is its name"</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 2:19)</span> (as is brought elsewhere) — in order to arouse the roots of their souls. And this is what is needed before the receiving of the Torah on <span class="key-term">Shavuos</span>, when the jealousy of the angels is aroused — just as they were jealous of Moshe when he ascended to receive the Torah, until Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> gave him the counsel to grasp himself in the <span class="key-term">Keesay HaKavod</span> etc., as is explained.</p>
<p>And therefore they were commanded to count them and bring them all in number and count — no man among them missing — in the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"He brings out their host by number; not one of them is missing"</span> <span class="source">(Isaiah 40:26)</span> — and to count them by their names specifically, in order to arouse all the roots of their souls, in order to be saved from the jealousy of the angels, as above. For this counting was commanded after the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> was erected — for everything is one aspect. For the construction of the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> was commanded to rectify the sin of the <span class="key-term">Aigel</span> [Golden Calf], as our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said. And the essential sin was drawn from the jealousy of the angels, who provoked against them so greatly for having received the Torah — until they caused them to sin. For even though Moshe Rabbeinu was saved from them by having grasped the <span class="key-term">Keesay HaKavod</span>, the rest of the Children of Israel were provoked against until they were caused to sin so greatly.</p>
<p>And therefore they were commanded to erect the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> and to count them all and that they should all travel around the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span>. For the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> with all its adornments and vessels, and all of Israel who traveled around it — all of it is in the aspect of the rectification of the <span class="key-term">Keesay HaKavod</span>, which is the illumination of the roots of the souls of Israel. For the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Bais HaMikdash</span>, which is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Keesay HaKavod</span>, as it is written: <span class="scripture">"A throne of glory, exalted from the beginning, is the place of our Sanctuary"</span> <span class="source">(Jeremiah 17:12)</span>. And everything is in order to rectify all of the above — to be saved from the jealousy of the angels by grasping ourselves, all of us, in the <span class="key-term">Keesay HaKavod</span>, which is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> and all its adornments, with all of Israel surrounding it, in the number of their names etc., as above. And therefore we read this parashah before <span class="key-term">Shavuos</span>, as above.</p>

<div class="section-header">§32</div>
<p>And this is the aspect of the journeys of the Children of Israel who traveled in the wilderness from encampment to encampment with the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> and its vessels and all of Israel surrounding it. And this is the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"From Hashem, the steps of a man are established, and He delights in his way"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 37:23)</span> — which is juxtaposed to the verse: <span class="scripture">"The wicked one borrows"</span> etc.</p>
<p>And this is the aspect of the great preciousness and virtue of the walking and traveling of Israel to synagogues and study halls and for the sake of a <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> — especially when one goes to draw close to the <span class="key-term">Tzadikay emes</span> who are in the aspect of <span class="key-term">M'komo shel Olam</span>. And this is the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"Our feet were standing within your gates, O Yerushalayim — Yerushalayim that is built"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 122:2–3)</span> etc. And this is the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"How beautiful are your feet in sandals… the curves of your thighs are like jewels"</span> <span class="source">(Song of Songs 7:2)</span> etc. — these are the <span class="key-term">shisin</span> [foundation pits] that are hollowed and bored down to the depths, the work of a Craftsman — the handiwork of the Creator. And this is when David dug the <span class="key-term">shisin</span> and the depths surged etc., and through this Achisofel was choked. And this is: <span class="scripture">"For as the heavens are high above the earth, so mighty is His kindness upon those who fear Him; as far as the east is from the west"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 103:11–12)</span> etc. — <span class="scripture">"For He knows our inclination; He remembers that we are dust"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 103:14)</span> — <span class="scripture">"and man, his days are like grass"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 103:15)</span> etc. — up to: <span class="scripture">"in all the places of His dominion"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 103:22)</span> etc. And this is: <span class="scripture">"Behold, how good and how pleasant it is for brothers to dwell together; like the good oil upon the head"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 133:1–2)</span> etc. — <span class="scripture">"like the dew of Chermon that descends upon the mountains of Tzion"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 133:3)</span> etc. And this is: <span class="scripture">"When you kindle the lamps, toward the face of the Menorah shall the seven lamps cast their light"</span> <span class="source">(Numbers 8:2)</span> etc. And this is the aspect of the 20th of Sivan. And this is why Parashas N'sachim is juxtaposed to Parashas HaM'raglim.</p>

<div class="section-header">§33</div>
<p>And to explain the matter: the general principle is that the essential greatness of man — that he can merit to have dominion over the angels — is because man descended to such a low and coarse place, which is the materiality of the dust, which is the ultimate degree of the materiality of place. And through this he has free will and many trials. And when he merits to rectify his deeds, then this man specifically is able to merit to ascend to the aspect of <span class="key-term">M'komo shel Olam</span> and to rule over the angels. And his essential merit and greatness is specifically because he is at the very ultimate degree of the materiality of place, as above.</p>
<p>And this <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> who merits this in truth is able to judge favorably even the entire world — because he knows the place of each and every one, through which he stumbled in what he stumbled in, <span class="key-term">chas v'shalom</span>. And he can bring him merit and elevate him from his place to the aspect of above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span>, and rectify everything — which is the aspect of his being able to make <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span> etc., as is explained and understood above.</p>
<p>And to merit all this is through grasping himself in the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Keesay HaKavod</span>, which is the roots of the souls of Israel — which is the aspect of the rectification of the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> and all the souls of Israel surrounding it etc., as above. For all the roots of the souls are drawn from the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> and the <span class="key-term">Bais HaMikdash</span>, which are the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Keesay HaKavod</span>, as above. And therefore the <span class="key-term">Bais HaMikdash</span> is called <span class="aramaic">"the chamber of the beds"</span> — on account of being the fruitfulness and multiplication of Israel, as our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said. That is, that from there all the roots of the souls are drawn, as above.</p>
<p>And this is the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"Behold, the bed of Shlomo"</span> <span class="source">(Song of Songs 3:7)</span> — and Rashi explained: this is the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span>. <span class="scripture">"Sixty mighty men surround it"</span> — Rashi explained that they are the six hundred thousand souls of the House of Israel. That is, as above — that all the six hundred thousand souls of Israel are drawn from the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> and the <span class="key-term">Bais HaMikdash</span>, for there is their root, as above. And this is: <span class="scripture">"All of them grasping swords, trained in war"</span> <span class="source">(Song of Songs 3:8)</span> etc. — for all the Children of Israel must be trained in war, to stand in the great battle of this world — that the jealousy of the angels should not cast them down, for from there come all the forces of the Evil Inclination of every person, as above. And therefore they are all called <span class="scripture">"those who go out to the army"</span> — as is explained in Parashas B'midbar — for every one of Israel, from small to great, is compelled and obligated to be a man of war, to go out in the army to fight the war of Hashem in this world. And the essential power is through connecting himself with the entirety of the souls of Israel, through the true <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> who is able to grasp himself in the <span class="key-term">Keesay HaKavod</span>, as above.</p>

<div class="section-header">§34</div>
<p>And this is the aspect of Israel traveling with the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> in all their journeys. For all the journeys of Israel in the wilderness with the <span class="key-term">Mishkan</span> were in order to elevate all the desolate places — a place of serpent, fiery serpent, and scorpion, and thirst — to elevate everything so that it would be included in the holiness of <span class="key-term">Eretz Yisroel</span>, where is the <span class="key-term">Bais HaMikdash</span> and the <span class="key-term">Kodesh Kodashim</span> [Holy of Holies], where the ladder is set upon the earth with its top reaching heaven — through which one is included in <span class="key-term">M'komo shel Olam</span>, as above.</p>
<p>And this is the aspect of all the exiles of Israel in general, and all the upheavals and journeys of each one of Israel in particular — everything is in order to elevate and raise all the places so that they will be included in the holiness of <span class="key-term">Eretz Yisroel</span>, so that they should ascend to the aspect of above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span> — which is the work of the Jew in this world, as above. And the essential thing is when a person walks or travels for the sake of a <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> — to synagogues, to study halls, and the like. And most essential of all is when he goes to draw close to the true <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> who is able to make <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span>, who is in the aspect of <span class="key-term">M'komo shel Olam</span>, as above. For then with every single step he rectifies a great deal — for he elevates and raises the <span class="key-term">Makom</span> to the aspect of above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span>, since he is going to draw close to the <span class="key-term">Tzadikim</span> who are in the aspect of <span class="key-term">M'komo shel Olam</span>, as above.</p>
<p>And this is the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"From Hashem, the steps of a man are established, and He delights in his way"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 37:23)</span> — which is juxtaposed to the verse: <span class="scripture">"The wicked one borrows and does not repay, but the Tzadik is gracious and gives"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 37:21)</span> — which speaks of the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> who is the great Lender, as above, who grasps the <span class="key-term">Keesay HaKavod</span>, through which he is included in the aspect of <span class="key-term">M'komo shel Olam</span>. And this is: <span class="scripture">"For those blessed by him shall inherit the land"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 37:22)</span> — certainly the aspect of <span class="scripture">"the righteous shall inherit the land"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 37:29)</span>. For all those who are close to him and blessed by him inherit the land — for they elevate the physical earth upward to the aspect of <span class="key-term">M'komo shel Olam</span>, as above. <span class="scripture">"And those cursed by him shall be cut off"</span> — for this is the essential <span class="key-term">karais</span> [excision], may the Merciful One save us, of those who sin in this world and not only that, but they also dispute the true <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> — to the point that they are among "those cursed by him," who are cut off from their root. For they remain buried in the materiality of the earth and are unable to elevate themselves upward — which is the essential secret of <span class="key-term">karais</span>, may the Merciful One save us, as is understood in the holy books.</p>
<p>And this is what is immediately juxtaposed: <span class="scripture">"From Hashem, the steps of a man are established, and He delights in his way."</span> For this is the essential trial of man in this world — that Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> established the steps of a man in this physical world. And there specifically "He delights in his way" — in which way he will walk. For the entire Torah is called by the name "way," as it is written: <span class="scripture">"In the entire way that Hashem your God has commanded you, you shall walk"</span> <span class="source">(Deuteronomy 5:30)</span>. And it is written: <span class="scripture">"For the ways of Hashem are straight, and the righteous walk in them, and the transgressors stumble in them"</span> <span class="source">(Hosea 14:10)</span>. And it is written: <span class="scripture">"For Hashem knows the way of the righteous, and the way of the wicked shall perish"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 1:6)</span>.</p>
<p>For this entire transient world is the aspect of a way — that man descended from a very, very lofty and exalted place to this world, to this physical dust — in order to walk and pass through it for the number of the days of his life. And there is the essential trial and the free will. And if he merits, he specifically will elevate the materiality of the place upward etc., as above. And this is: "From Hashem, the steps of a man are established" — that every single step that a person treads with his feet is all established by Hashem. For Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, Who is above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span>, established in His wonders the steps of a man in this physical dust, which is the ultimate degree of <span class="key-term">Makom</span> — which He, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, contracted through many contractions from the aspect of above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span> until He created this physical <span class="key-term">Makom</span> to establish in it the steps of a man. And specifically through the steps of a man in this physical dust, which is the ultimate degree of <span class="key-term">Makom</span> — through this specifically must the physical person cleave to Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> and to His holy Torah, until he ascends from the materiality of the <span class="key-term">Makom</span> specifically to the aspect of above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span>. And this is because here, in this physical dust, is the essential <span class="key-term">b'chirah</span> [free will], as above.</p>
<p>And this is: "And He delights in his way" — that Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> established the steps of a man, and there is the essential <span class="key-term">b'chirah</span>, the aspect of "He delights in his way" — that Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> delights in the way of man, that he himself specifically should choose and select for himself the good way, as above. And this is the aspect of what we wrote above — that the entire discharge of debts to Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> is impossible except when all the powers of abundance reach this physical dust specifically. For everything that Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> created from beginning to end is all in order that all the powers of vitality and abundance should be drawn down to the very ultimate degree of the materiality of <span class="key-term">Makom</span>. And from there specifically, from the very ultimate degree of the materiality of <span class="key-term">Makom</span>, they should ascend higher and higher until they ascend to the aspect of above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span>, as above. And therefore "From Hashem, the steps of a man are established" etc. is juxtaposed to the verse "The [wicked] one borrows" etc. (as above). Understand this very, very well.</p>

<div class="section-header">§35</div>
<p>And this is: <span class="scripture">"For as the heavens are high above the earth, so mighty is His kindness upon those who fear Him"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 103:11)</span>. "As the heavens are high above the earth" specifically — just so "His kindness is mighty" etc. For it has already been explained that the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> who is included in the aspect of above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span> draws a wondrous kindness, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span> — to judge each one favorably according to his place, because he knows the place of each and every one: to where he descended, and from where he was drawn etc. And this is: "For as the heavens are high above the earth" — because Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> knows how very, very high the heavens are above the earth, and this man descended from such an exalted place to such a physical place, which is the dust of the earth. And therefore, because of this itself, "His kindness is mighty" etc. For through this is the essential drawing of the kindness of the true <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> — because man descended from the heavens to the earth, as above.</p>
<p>And this is: "His kindness is mighty upon those who fear Him" — for this kindness is drawn specifically by the God-fearing ones, who are the <span class="key-term">Tzadikay emes</span> who merit to grasp the <span class="key-term">Keesay HaKavod</span>, through which they are able to ascend above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span> and to make <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span>, as above. For all of this depends upon <span class="key-term">yirah</span>, which is the aspect of the building of <span class="key-term">Yerushalayim</span> — as is explained at the beginning of the aforementioned Torah. See there well. It emerges that the verse connects beautifully: "For as the heavens are high above the earth" — just so, precisely, "His kindness is mighty upon those who fear Him." For specifically through this, the God-fearing ones who ascend above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span> draw the aforementioned wondrous kindness of <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span> — because man descended from the heavens, which are so very high, to this lowly, physical earth, as above.</p>
<p>And it is not enough for us that [we descended to] this physical place — but also within this physical place He has scattered us to the four corners of the earth, and we are so very far from the place of our Sanctuary. And Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> in His mercy knows how many transgressions people came to on account of this scattering to such distant places. And through this the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> arouses great kindnesses and mercies to forgive all our transgressions and to bring us into the category of merit and to return us in <span class="key-term">t'shuvah</span> to Him, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>.</p>
<p>And this is: <span class="scripture">"As far as the east is from the west, He has distanced our transgressions from us"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 103:12)</span> — and it is understood by the wise person on his own. For through this very distance — that the east is far from the west, and Israel are scattered across all four corners in the east and in the west etc. — through this itself "He has distanced our transgressions from us," as above.</p>
<p>And this is: <span class="scripture">"For He knows our inclination; He remembers that we are dust"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 103:14)</span> — "dust" specifically. For He, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, remembers that we descended from the heavens to the dust of this physical earth, where are all the forces of the evil inclinations. And through this He judges us with mercy and great kindness — because He knows our inclination, by remembering that we are dust — that we descended to such a physical place, from the heavens to the earth etc., as above.</p>
<p>And this is: <span class="scripture">"Man — his days are like grass, like a blossom of the field, so he blossoms"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 103:15)</span> etc. For man is truly like grass and like the blossom and growth of the field — whose entire purpose is realized when there is a <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> who is able to elevate all their powers upward, in the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"And Yitzchak went out to converse in the field"</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 24:63)</span>, as above. And this is all the work of man in this world, as above.</p>
<p>And this is: <span class="scripture">"For a wind passes through him and he is gone, and his place knows him no more"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 103:16)</span>. And it is expounded in two ways. For one who does not merit — it is said about him literally: that a slight wind passes over a person and he becomes nothing and naught, like dried grass and a wilted blossom, and the place of his wealth and greatness in which he spread himself in this world is no longer recognizable — as though he would live forever. And as King David, <em>alav hashalom</em>, and all the <span class="key-term">Tzadikim</span> cry out about this in many expressions, as it is written: <span class="scripture">"For in his death he will not take everything"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 49:18)</span> etc. — <span class="scripture">"Their inner thought is that their houses will last forever"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 49:12)</span> etc. — <span class="scripture">"And man, despite his honor, does not abide"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 49:13)</span> etc. And likewise many more.</p>
<p>But if he merits to do well in his deeds, then he elevates all the powers to Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> and ascends specifically from the materiality of this <span class="key-term">Makom</span> to the aspect of above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span>, and is included in the <span class="key-term">Ain Sof</span> [the Infinite]. And about him the verse is interpreted favorably: "For a wind passes through him and he is gone" — that the breath of life passes through man specifically, upon whom everything depends. And through this "he is gone" — that he is included in the <span class="key-term">Ain Sof</span>. And this is: "And his place knows him no more" — for the <span class="key-term">Makom</span> is included in the aspect of above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span>, for solely for this did he descend to this physical <span class="key-term">Makom</span>, as above.</p>
<p>And this is: <span class="scripture">"And the kindness of Hashem is from everlasting to everlasting upon those who fear Him"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 103:17)</span> — that is, the aspect of the aforementioned kindness that is drawn by the God-fearing ones who ascend above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span>, as above. And this is how [the psalm] concludes: <span class="scripture">"Bless Hashem, His angels, mighty in strength, who do His word, to hear"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 103:20)</span> etc. — who are the <span class="key-term">Tzadikim</span>, who are mighty in strength, who merit the Word of Hashem, the aspect of "who do His word" etc. — until: <span class="scripture">"Bless Hashem, all His works, in all the places of His dominion"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 103:22)</span> etc. "In all the places" specifically — for the <span class="key-term">Tzadikim</span> elevate all the places to His dominion, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, until everything is included in the aspect of above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span>, as above.</p>
<p>And this is what is mentioned there earlier: <span class="scripture">"Hashem has established His throne in the heavens, and His sovereignty rules over all"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 103:19)</span>. For this is the essential <span class="key-term">tikkun</span> — through the <span class="key-term">Keesay HaKavod</span> that the <span class="key-term">Tzadikim</span> grasp, through which they elevate everything — until they draw His sovereignty and dominion over the entire world, the aspect of "and His sovereignty rules over all," as above. And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span>, when we engage in revealing His sovereignty over the entire world — as our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, established for us to pray extensively about this: <span class="scripture">"And You shall reign"</span> etc. and <span class="scripture">"Reign"</span> etc. — until: "And let everything that has breath in its nostrils say: Hashem, the God of Israel, is King, and His sovereignty rules over all" — and they conclude: <span class="scripture">"King over all the earth"</span> specifically. For this is His essential greatness, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, as above.</p>

<div class="section-header">§36</div>
<p>And this is the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"Our feet were standing within your gates, O Yerushalayim"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 122:2)</span>. For it has already been explained that the essential ascent from the <span class="key-term">Makom</span> to the aspect of above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span> is in <span class="key-term">Yerushalayim</span>, where is the <span class="key-term">Bais HaMikdash</span>, as above. And a person must intend, in every place where he has been scattered, in every place where he sets his feet — as though he is standing in <span class="key-term">Yerushalayim</span>. For solely for this was he scattered and exiled there — in order that he should elevate and raise this physical and very distant place to the aspect of <span class="key-term">Yerushalayim</span> and the <span class="key-term">Bais HaMikdash</span>, where one ascends above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span>, as above. And this is through remembering Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> in every place where his foot treads — all the more so when he merits to pray and pour out his conversation there. For all the services and prayers must ascend by way of <span class="key-term">Eretz Yisroel</span>, in the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"And they shall pray to You by way of their land"</span> <span class="source">(I Kings 8:48)</span>.</p>
<p>And this is: "Our feet were standing within your gates, O Yerushalayim" — that every place where our feet stand is "within your gates, O Yerushalayim," as above. And this is: <span class="scripture">"Yerushalayim that is built like a city that is joined together"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 122:3)</span> — which is the <span class="key-term">Yerushalayim</span> of above, which is above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span>. For through <span class="key-term">Yerushalayim</span> one ascends from the physical <span class="key-term">Makom</span> to above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span>. And this is: <span class="scripture">"For there the tribes ascended, the tribes of God… to give thanks to the Name of Hashem"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 122:4)</span> etc. — that is, thanksgiving and prayer, the aspect of the Word of Hashem — which is the essential thing, through which one ascends from the <span class="key-term">Makom</span> to above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span>, as above.</p>
<p>And this is: <span class="scripture">"For there the thrones sat for judgment"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 122:5)</span> etc. "For there" specifically the thrones sit for judgment — for there they judge and adjudicate everyone favorably, because it is above the <span class="key-term">Makom</span>, through which is the essential kindness — to judge everything with kindness according to his place — which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span>, as above. And this is: <span class="scripture">"Thrones for judgment, thrones for the House of David"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 122:5)</span>. David is <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span>, as it is written: <span class="scripture">"the anointed of the God of Yaakov"</span> <span class="source">(II Samuel 23:1)</span>. That is, the thrones for judgment are solely for merit and kindness, in the aspect of "thrones for the House of David" — which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span>, who merits all the aforementioned rectifications until he merits to make <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span>, to judge everyone favorably according to his place — because he is the aspect of <span class="key-term">M'komo shel Olam</span>, through grasping himself in the <span class="key-term">Keesay HaKavod</span>, as above.</p>

<div class="translator-summary">
<h3>Summary of Uraiv 5 — Part 2 (§§30–36)</h3>
<p>§30: All kinyanim learned from "v'zos l'fanim b'Yisroel...shalaf ish na'alo" (Rus 4:7) = when Boaz married Rus → David → Mashiach → rectifies all, elevates to shoresh (emes, no shinui) → source of kinyanim.</p>
<p>§31: Before Shavuos → read Parashas B'midbar (counting Yisroel when Mishkan erected). "M'nu v'itzru." Each year = draw k'dushas kabbalas haTorah on Shavuos → tachlis = memshalah on mal'achim. But need kiyum → grasp Keesay HaKavod = shoreshay nishmos Yisroel. Therefore count them b'mispar shaimos (shaim = nefesh, "nefesh chayah hu sh'mo") → arouse shoreshay nafshos → saved from kin'as hamal'achim. Counting after Mishkan erected (= tikkun chait ha'Aigel, Chazal). Chait from kin'as hamal'achim → Moshe saved (grasped Keesay HaKavod) but rest of Yisroel sinned. Commanded: erect Mishkan + count all + travel s'viv → all = tikkun Keesay HaKavod = he'aras shoreshay nafshos Yisroel. Mishkan = Bais HaMikdash = Keesay HaKavod ("Keesay kavod marom meirishon m'kom mikdashainu," Yirmiyah 17:12). Therefore read this parashah before Shavuos.</p>
<p>§32: Journeys of B'nay Yisroel in midbar with Mishkan = "maiHashem mitz'aday gever konanu v'darko yechpatz" (T'hilim 37:23, juxtaposed to "loveh rasha"). G'dolah of halichas Yisroel to batay k'nesiyos/batay midrashis/d'var mitzvah, especially to Tzadikay emes = M'komo shel Olam. References: "omdos hayu raglaynu bish'arayich Yerushalayim" (T'hilim 122:2); "mah yafu f'amayich ban'alim...chamukay y'raichayich" (Shir HaShirim 7:2) = shisin (bored to t'hom, ma'asay y'day Oman); David dug shisin → t'homa → Achisofel choked. "Ki chig'voha shamayim al ha'aretz gavar chasdo...ki hu yada yitzrainu zachur ki afar anachnu...enosh kechatzir yamav" → "b'chol m'komos memshalso." "Hinay mah tov...kashe'men hatov" (T'hilim 133). "B'ha'aloscha es haneiros" (B'midbar 8:2). B'chinas 20 Sivan. Parashas N'sachim juxtaposed to HaM'raglim.</p>
<p>§33: K'lal: man's greatness (memshalah over mal'achim) = because descended to low, coarse place (gashmiyus he'afar = tachlis gashmiyus haMakom) → b'chirah + nisyonos → when rectifies → ascends to M'komo shel Olam → rules over mal'achim. Merit specifically from tachlis gashmiyus. Tzadik who merits = dan l'chaf z'chus entire world (knows m'kom kol echad) → elevates from m'komo to l'maalah min haMakom → Rosh HaShanah. Through grasping Keesay HaKavod = shoreshay nishmos = tikkun Mishkan with all Yisroel s'vivav. Bais HaMikdash = "chadar hamitos" (piryan v'rivyan shel Yisroel, Chazal = from there all shoreshay n'shamos). "Hinay mitaso shel Shlomo" (Shir HaShirim 3:7, Rashi = Mishkan). "Shishim giborim saviv lah" (Rashi = 600K nafshos Bais Yisroel). "Kulam achuzay cherev m'lumday milchamah" = all Yisroel must be trained for milchamah (lest kin'as hamal'achim cast them down). All called "yotz'ay tzava" (Parashas B'midbar). Ikkar ko'ach through connecting to k'lal nafshos Yisroel through Tzadik ha'emes → Keesay HaKavod.</p>
<p>§34: Journeys with Mishkan in midbar = elevate desolate places (nachash saraf v'akrav v'tzima'on) → included in k'dushas Eretz Yisroel (Bais HaMikdash, Kodesh Kodashim, sulam mutzav artzah v'rosho magia hashamaymah → niklalim b'M'komo shel Olam). All galuyos + tilulim = elevate m'komos → k'dushas Eretz Yisroel → l'maalah min haMakom. Ikkar: going l'dvar mitzvah, batay k'nesiyos, especially l'hiskareiv l'Tzadik ha'emes (= M'komo shel Olam) → each step rectifies greatly. "MaiHashem mitz'aday gever konanu v'darko yechpatz" juxtaposed to "loveh rasha...v'Tzadik chonaim v'no'sain." "Ki m'vorachav yirshu aretz" = Tzadikim yirshu aretz (elevate gashmiyus ha'aretz l'maalah). "Um'kulalav yikaraisu" = ikkar karais (disputing Tzadik → cut from shoresh → buried in gashmiyus). All of this olam = derech ("b'chol haderech asher tzivah Hashem...tailaichu," D'varim 5:30; "y'sharim darchay Hashem," Hoshaia 14:10; "ki yodai'a Hashem derech Tzadikim," T'hilim 1:6). Man descended from high place to afar → ikkar nisayon/b'chirah → if merits: elevates gashmiyus haMakom l'maalah. Every step "konanu maiHashem" (from l'maalah min haMakom → tzimtzumim → created Makom hagashmi → haichain bo mitz'aday gever). Through steps in afar → cleave to Hashem/Torah → ascend from gashmiyus haMakom to l'maalah. Ikkar siluk chovos = when ko'chos hashefa reach afar → ascend from tachlis gashmiyus l'maalah. Understand well.</p>
<p>§35: "Ki chig'voha shamayim al ha'aretz gavar chasdo al y'raiav" (T'hilim 103:11). "Chig'voha shamayim al ha'aretz" davka = Tzadik niklal b'l'maalah min haMakom draws chesed nifla = Rosh HaShanah (dan l'z'chus l'fi m'komo, knows where each descended from). Because descended from shamayim to afar → gavar chasdo. "Al y'raiav" = y'raiim = Tzadikay emes grasping Keesay HaKavod → above Makom → Rosh HaShanah. All depends on yirah = binyan Yerushalayim. Scattered to 4 corners → Tzadik arouses chasadim/rachamim to forgive. "Kirchok mizrach mima'arav hirchik mimenu es p'sha'aynu" (T'hilim 103:12) = through distance itself → removes transgressions. "Ki hu yada yitzraynu zachur ki afar anachnu" = remembers we descended to afar → judges with rachamim. "Enosh kechatzir yamav k'tzitz hasadeh kain yatzitz" = man like grass/blossom → tachlis when Tzadik elevates ("vayaitzai Yitzchak lasuach basadeh"). "Ki ru'ach avrah bo v'ainenu v'lo yakirenu od m'komo" (T'hilim 103:16) = two interpretations: (1) negative = wind passes → nothing remains of wealth/greatness; (2) positive = ru'ach chayim passes through man → "ainenu" = niklal b'Ain Sof; "lo yakirenu od m'komo" = Makom niklal b'l'maalah min haMakom. "V'chesed Hashem mai'olam v'ad olam al y'raiav" = chesed drawn by y'raiim above Makom. "Bar'chu Hashem mal'achav giboray ko'ach osay d'varo" = Tzadikim giboray ko'ach, osay d'varo → "b'chol m'komos memshalso" = Tzadikim elevate all m'komos. "Hashem bashamayim haichin kis'o umalchuso bakol mashalah" = tikkun through Keesay HaKavod → draw malchuso bakol = Rosh HaShanah (t'filos: "v'simloch...u'mloch...Hashem Elokay Yisroel Melech umalchuso bakol mashalah...Melech al kol ha'aretz" davka).</p>
<p>§36: "Omdos hayu raglaynu bish'arayich Yerushalayim." Ikkar aliyah from Makom to l'maalah min haMakom = in Yerushalayim (Bais HaMikdash). Must intend in every place: as if standing in Yerushalayim. Scattered only to elevate that distant Makom to Yerushalayim/Bais HaMikdash. Through remembering Hashem wherever he treads → especially meriting to pray there. All avodos/t'filos ascend through Eretz Yisroel ("v'hisp'al'lu ailecha derech artzam," M'lachim I 8:48). "Omdos hayu raglaynu bish'arayich Yerushalayim" = every place our feet stand = your gates, Yerushalayim. "Yerushalayim hab'nuyah k'ir shechubrah lah yachdav" = Yerushalayim shel maalah = l'maalah min haMakom. "Shesham alu sh'vatim shivtay Kah...l'hodos l'shaim Hashem" = hodayah/t'filah = d'var Hashem → ascend. "Ki shamah yashvu kis'os l'mishpat" = there davka → judge l'z'chus (above Makom → chesed → Rosh HaShanah). "Kis'os l'mishpat kis'os l'vais David" = David = Mashiach ("M'shiach Elokay Yaakov") → kis'os solely for z'chus/chesed → all tikkunim → Rosh HaShanah → dan hakol l'z'chus l'fi m'komo → M'komo shel Olam → Keesay HaKavod.</p>
<p class="translator-note">Based on Likutay Moharan II:1 "Teekoo" · Hilchos Uraiv 5 — Part 2 (§§30–36). Part 3 continues with §§37–46.</p>
